<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Togo</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Togo is a country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-guinea">Gulf of Guinea</a>. As Togoland it was a German colony from the 1880s until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, when it was occupied by British and French forces. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> list it among the modest German colonial possessions stripped by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> and redistributed as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations-mandate">League of Nations mandates</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/togo.jpg" alt="Togo" width="320" height="198" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: See File history below for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Togo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Togo is a small tropical country of 56,785 square kilometres, less than 115 kilometres wide between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a>. It extends north from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-guinea">Gulf of Guinea</a>, where its coast is 56 kilometres long and consists of lagoons with sandy beaches, to the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burkina-faso">Burkina Faso</a>. The south has a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savannah">savannah</a> and woodland plateau reaching a coastal plain with lagoons and marshes, the centre is hilly, and the north is rolling savannah. The highest mountain is Mont Agou at 986 metres, and the longest river is the Mono, at 400 kilometres.</p><p>The name Togo means 'by the water' or 'behind the sea', from the Ewe words to, 'water', and go, 'shore'. It originally referred only to the town of Togo, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/togoville">Togoville</a>, and the Germans later extended it to the whole country.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Archaeological finds indicate that societies in the area could produce pottery and process iron. Between the 11th and 16th centuries the Ewe entered the area from the west, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meena">Mina</a> and Gun from the east, and most settled on the coast. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a> began in the 16th century. For the next two hundred years the coastal region was a trading centre for Europeans in search of slaves, which earned Togo and the surrounding region the name the Slave Coast.</p><p>In 1884 a paper was forcibly signed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/togoville">Togoville</a> with King Mlapa III, whereby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> claimed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> over a stretch of coast and gradually extended its control inland. In 1905 this became the German colony of Togoland. The local population was forced to work, to cultivate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> and cocoa, and to pay taxes, and a railway and the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lome">Lomé</a> were built for export.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> invaded in August 1914, and German forces surrendered on 26 August. The territory was partitioned into British and French zones, which on 20 July 1922 became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations-mandate">League of Nations mandates</a>. French Togoland became an autonomous republic in 1956, and British Togoland voted to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a>, as part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>, in 1957. Togo became independent from France in 1960.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Togo is the Togolese Republic, with its capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lome">Lomé</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-guinea">Gulf of Guinea</a>. French is the official language, and Kabiye and Ewe are national languages. The population is about 8 million, with some 40 ethnic groups, of which the Ewe in the south are the most numerous. Culture draws on Ewe and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meena">Mina</a> traditions in the south, including the creator god Mawu and the vodun, and on Kabye and Tem traditions in the north, including the Evala wrestling initiation rite.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Togo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The First Sino-Japanese War of 1894 to 1895 and the Russo-Japanese War, which culminated in Admiral <b>Togo</b>'s annihilation of the Russian fleet at Tsushima in May 1905, showed a consistent Japanese operational method: rapid offensive tempo, tactical aggression, and the exploitation of enemy hesitation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Its colonial empire was modest, comprising German East Africa, German South West Africa, <b>Togo</b>, Cameroon, the leased territory of Kiautschou in China, and Pacific island territories, all stripped by the Treaty of Versailles and largely redistributed as League of Nations mandates.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-empire">German Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>At its height the kingdom covered the Northern, Upper West, Upper East, Savannah and North East regions of present-day Ghana and parts of Burkina Faso, north-eastern Ivory Coast and north-western <b>Togo</b>, and Islam entered northern Ghana through its court and the Dyula and Hausa merchants it welcomed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-dagbon">Kingdom of Dagbon · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Yoruba are one of the largest peoples of Africa, today more than forty million strong, living mainly in south-western Nigeria and in neighbouring Benin and <b>Togo</b>.</blockquote><a href="/religion/yoruba-religion">Yoruba religion · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Anlo tradition traces its people to Notsie, in modern <b>Togo</b>, from which the Ewe escaped the tyranny of King Agokoli and dispersed, probably in the 17th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/anlo">Anlo</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Togo</b> became independent on 27 April 1960, and Olympio was assassinated in 1963.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-togoland">French Togoland</a></li></ul></section>
